  <section id="interview-prep">
    <p class="eyebrow">Get ready</p>
    <h2>Interview prep specifics for entry-level BA roles</h2>
    <p>
      BA interviews in India typically run across several distinct rounds, and each one tests something different.
    </p>

    <div class="work-style-grid">
      {interviewCards.map((card) => (
        <article class="work-style-card">
          <p class="mini-label">{card.label}</p>
          <h3>{card.title}</h3>
          <p>{card.body}</p>
        </article>
      ))}
    </div>

    <p>
      Case-study exercises in BA interviews commonly ask you to identify the real problem, the stakeholders, the assumptions, the risks, and a first-draft set of requirements or user stories from a short, messy business scenario.
    </p>
    <p>
      If you have already built the mock project described above, this round stops being a cold exercise and becomes a rehearsed skill.
    </p>
    <p>
      Behavioural questions carry real weight too — expect to explain a time you resolved conflicting information from different people, and to explain your mock project clearly in under two minutes without reading from notes.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="gates">
    <p class="eyebrow">Second filter</p>
    <h2>The 3 Gates before you commit hard to this route</h2>
    <p>
      After running your plan through <strong>The 4-Checkpoint Protocol</strong>, test it through <strong>The 3 Gates</strong>.
    </p>

    <div class="gates-block">
      <p class="gates-block__title">A route is stronger when it passes all three gates, not just the excitement of the job title.</p>
