  <section id="next-step">
    <p class="eyebrow">Action plan</p>
    <h2>What to do next if you are serious about becoming a business analyst</h2>
    <p>
      First, pick one real, observable process and build the mock project — BRD, process map, user stories, and a stakeholder interview writeup.
    </p>
    <p>
      Second, decide whether ECBA is worth adding as a signal alongside that project, based on how thin your resume currently looks without it.
    </p>
    <p>
      Third, target IT services and banking first for the widest entry-level hiring volume, and treat product-company roles as a stretch goal once you have SQL and dashboard comfort to go with your requirement-gathering proof.
    </p>
    <p>
      Fourth, practice explaining your mock project out loud, clearly, in under two minutes — that single habit prepares you for most of what the interview rounds above actually test.
    </p>
    <p>
      Moving toward earlier financial freedom on this path comes down to the same thing it always does:
      a genuine <strong>high-income skill portfolio</strong> built on visible proof, not a stack of course
      completions. If you want a second opinion on whether business analysis genuinely fits your background,
      <a href="https://futurecareerschool.com/services/career-counselling-and-career-guidance/">career guidance</a>
      can help you map the entry path that fits your situation, or start with the free
      <a href="https://futurecareerschool.com/services/assessments">career and skill assessments</a> if you are
      still deciding whether this is your lane. If you are still unsure this is the right lane for you, revisit
      the
      <a href="/blog/skills/business-analyst-vs-data-analyst-career-india/">business analyst vs data analyst comparison</a>
      or explore the wider decision tools on the <a href="https://futurecareerschool.com/career-skills-compass" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Career & Skills Compass</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

